SOLUTION:
Reasoning and generalizable method:
- Model each friend as a task with a location, availability window [start, end], and a required minimum meeting duration.
- You start at Richmond District at 9:00AM and can travel between neighborhoods using the given (asymmetric) travel times. You are allowed to wait at any location to start a meeting when the friend becomes available.
- For any candidate visiting order, build the earliest-feasible schedule greedily:
  - From your current time and location, compute the arrival time at the next friend’s location.
  - The meeting starts at the later of the arrival time and the friend’s window start; if the meeting can finish by the window end, schedule it (including any necessary waiting), update current time and location, and continue. Otherwise, skip that friend in this order and consider the next.
  - Waiting at a friend’s location is never worse than waiting elsewhere when travel times are time-invariant.
- Search over all permutations of friends (small problem size), constructing a feasible subsequence for each order as above (skipping infeasible tasks on the fly). Pick the plan that meets the maximum number of friends. Break ties by earliest finishing time, then minimal total waiting, then minimal total travel.
- This method is general for small to moderate problem sizes; for larger instances, use heuristic search or MILP/CP solvers.

